+++ b/engdocs/plans/vp-petx-bisect-serialization-failure.md
@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+# Plan: vp-petx — Bisect fork-delta serialization failure
+
+## Problem
+
+`TestSelectOrCreatePoolSessionBead_SerializesAliasCheckAndCreate` fails 5/5 on `fork/main` but passes 5/5 on `origin/main` under identical conditions. Every directly-involved symbol (test function, `blockingPoolCreateStore` fake, `selectOrCreatePoolSessionBead`, `createPoolSessionBeadWithGuardedAlias`, `WithCitySessionIdentifierLocks`, `withCitySessionIdentifierLock`, `internal/session/names.go`, `agent_build_params.go`) is **byte-identical** between branches. The fork carries 50 commits ahead of origin.
+
+### Failure symptom
+
+```
+second pool create did not start after first create completed
+```
+
+The test races two goroutines calling `selectOrCreatePoolSessionBead`. The first acquires a `syscall.Flock` file lock on alias `"claude-1"` via `WithCitySessionIdentifierLocks`, then blocks inside `store.Create()` on `<-releaseFirstCreate`. The second goroutine should block at the `Flock` call, then proceed after the first releases. Instead, the second goroutine **never reaches `store.Create()`** after the first completes.
+
+### Root cause hypothesis
+
+The fork's 50-commit delta introduces a package-level side effect (an `init()` change in `dolt_scope_watchdog.go` / `dolt_start_managed.go` — 279 lines changed between those two files alone, or a transitive import that alters behavior) that interferes with the file-lock serialization path or the goroutine scheduling before the second goroutine reaches `Flock`. The direct call chain is untouched.
+
+### Why bisect, not static analysis
+
+With 50 commits and no changes to the direct call chain, the interaction is transitive. A `git bisect` on the single test is the fastest way to isolate the breaking commit. Static analysis of all 50 commits' transitive effects would take longer than a 6-step bisect.
+
+## Plan
+
+| ID | Description | Acceptance | est_min | slings |
+|----|-------------|------------|---------|--------|
+| T-001 | Write a failing reproduction script that runs `TestSelectOrCreatePoolSessionBead_SerializesAliasCheckAndCreate` 5× on the current branch and asserts at least one failure | Script exits 0 if it captures a failure, 1 if all 5 pass (wrong branch or test was fixed) | 3 | |
+| T-002 | Run `git bisect` between `origin/main` (good) and `fork/main` (bad), using the reproduction script from T-001 as the bisect runner, to isolate the breaking commit | Bisect completes and reports a single culpable commit SHA | 5 | |
+| T-003 | Read the culpable commit diff in full, trace how its changes reach the `selectOrCreatePoolSessionBead` / `WithCitySessionIdentifierLocks` / `Flock` path, and write a root-cause analysis as a `bd comment` on vp-petx | Comment posted on vp-petx with: (1) the breaking commit SHA, (2) the specific code change, (3) the mechanism by which it breaks serialization, (4) proposed fix (fork-specific guard, upstream revert, or init-order fix) | 4 | |
+| T-004 | Implement the fix identified in T-003 — apply the minimal change to fork/main that restores the test (guard the init side-effect, add a missing unlock, or revert the culpable commit) | `TestSelectOrCreatePoolSessionBead_SerializesAliasCheckAndCreate` passes 5/5 on the fix branch | 4 | |
+| T-005 | Run the full `cmd/gc` test suite (`go test ./cmd/gc/ -count=1 -timeout=300s`) to confirm the fix doesn't regress any other test | Full suite green, 0 failures | 5 | |
+
+## GDPR data-flow impact
+
+None. This is a test-only change in the gascity build/desired-state reconciliation code. No user data, no PII, no cross-border data flows.
+
+## MDR Class I traceability
+
+No-op. This change is outside the voxmemo clinical pipeline. No clinical data, no chain-of-evidence impact.
